{"id": "219360", "revid": "36077", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219360", "title": "EuroVelo 10", "text": "EuroVelo 10 (Baltic Sea Cycle Route) is an international long-distance cycle route in the EuroVelo network. It forms a circuit around the Baltic Sea. The official loop is about 9,100 km long and passes through nine countries: Denmark, Sweden, Finland, Russia, Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania, Poland and Germany. There is no prescribed starting point or direction of travel.\nThe route links major port cities, resorts, islands, forests, farmland and long stretches of coast. Its standard varies greatly: paved cycleways alternate with gravel, forest roads, concrete slabs, cobbles and ordinary public roads. The official track also includes sections that are still being developed or planned.\nUnderstand.\n&lt;mapframe latitude=\"58.4\" longitude=\"19.5\" zoom=\"4\" height=\"500\" width=\"700\" align=\"center\" text=\"EuroVelo 10 route according to OpenStreetMap\" show=\"evbase\" /&gt;\nEV10 route available in OSM data: \u2501\u2501 all mapped sections.\n\"On each country map, that country's section of EV10 is highlighted with a thicker red line, while the other mapped sections remain yellow. The lines are display-optimised geometry from OpenStreetMap super-relation 2689634, retrieved through Waymarked Trails on 31 July 2026. The Russian sections of the formal route are not included in that super-relation and are therefore not shown. The maps do not replace a current GPX track or navigation app: OSM is community-maintained, and ferry links and unfinished sections may not form a continuous line.\"\nKey facts.\nThe official EuroVelo site provides a full track and a separate track containing only developed, signposted or certified sections. The full GPX does not imply that there is a finished, continuous cycleway.\n\"The distances below are approximate values calculated from the official 2026 GPX tracks. They exclude ferry distances; the additional Bornholm circuit is included under Denmark. Their sum therefore differs from the official 9,100 km.\"\nOpenStreetMap route maps.\nIn OSM, EV10 is stored as super-relation 2689634, whose members include national relations. Waymarked Trails highlights the selected route against other cycle routes:\n\"OSM relations show the state of a community-maintained map, not formal certification or a guarantee that a section is rideable. Compare them with the official GPX and notices from regional route managers before travelling.\"\nSurfaces and traffic.\nA survey of 2,637 km of southern EV10, carried out in 2024\u20132025, found that 53% of the surveyed route was free of motor traffic, 27% used roads with very low traffic and 12% used roads with low traffic. Fully separated infrastructure accounted for 47%, and 82% of surfaces were rated good or very good to ride. These figures concern surveyed sections in Denmark, Germany, Poland, Lithuania and southern Sweden, not the full 9,100 km.\nCommon difficulties include:\nOn Velo Baltica in West Pomerania, 73% of the route is free of motor traffic, 59% is asphalt and 25% is made of pavers, slabs or cobbles. On the Pomeranian and Warmian-Masurian section, asphalt accounts for 61%, stabilised gravel 18%, pavers, slabs or cobbles 15%, and earth or loose gravel 6%.\nWaymarking.\nThe recommended EuroVelo sign shows the number 10 on a blue field surrounded by yellow stars. In practice, also look for national route markings:\nPrepare.\nSample plan for the full circuit.\nUntil the Russian borders reopen, the full official circuit cannot be ridden without a ferry, train or unofficial diversion.\nGet in.\nThe most convenient places to start or end are major rail and ferry hubs. Check timetables and bicycle-carriage conditions immediately before travelling.\n\"The following lists successive route nodes from the official GPX. Intermediate villages are also listed for Poland. EV10 often skirts settlements rather than passing through their centres; use a current track for navigation.\"\nRoute.\nDetailed route by country.\nSweden.\n&lt;mapframe latitude=\"62.5\" longitude=\"16.5\" zoom=\"4\" height=\"420\" width=\"700\" align=\"center\" text=\"EV10 in Sweden \u2014 national section highlighted\" show=\"evbase,evsweden\" /&gt;\nHighlight: \u2501\u2501 Swedish section \u00b7 \u2501\u2501 other mapped EV10 sections.\nMalm\u00f6 \u2013 Skan\u00f6r \u2013 Trelleborg \u2013 Smygehuk \u2013 Ystad \u2013 Simrishamn \u2013 Br\u00f6sarp \u2013 Kristianstad \u2013 S\u00f6lvesborg \u2013 Karlshamn \u2013 Karlskrona \u2013 Tors\u00e5s \u2013 Kalmar/Smedby \u2013 M\u00f6nster\u00e5s \u2013 Oskarshamn \u2013 Figeholm \u2013 Blankaholm \u2013 V\u00e4stervik \u2013 Loftahammar \u2013 Valdemarsvik \u2013 S\u00f6derk\u00f6ping \u2013 Nyk\u00f6ping \u2013 Vagnh\u00e4rad \u2013 Stockholm \u2013 \u00c5kersberga \u2013 Gavel \u2013 \u00d6sterbybruk \u2013 Marma \u2013 G\u00e4vle \u2013 Bergby \u2013 S\u00f6derhamn \u2013 Hudiksvall \u2013 Harm\u00e5nger \u2013 Armsj\u00f6torpet \u2013 Sundsvall \u2013 Bergeforsen \u2013 H\u00e4rn\u00f6sand \u2013 Ull\u00e5nger \u2013 \u00d6rnsk\u00f6ldsvik \u2013 Husum \u2013 Nordmaling \u2013 Ume\u00e5 \u2013 Robertsfors \u2013 L\u00f6v\u00e5nger \u2013 Skellefte\u00e5 \u2013 Byske \u2013 Pite\u00e5 \u2013 Lule\u00e5 \u2013 T\u00f6re \u2013 Kalix \u2013 Haparanda.\nFrom Ystad, a ferry reaches Danish Bornholm, where an additional circuit runs R\u00f8nne \u2013 Allinge \u2013 Svaneke \u2013 Dueodde \u2013 R\u00f8nne, about 103 km.\nFinland.\n&lt;mapframe latitude=\"62.5\" longitude=\"25.5\" zoom=\"4\" height=\"420\" width=\"700\" align=\"center\" text=\"EV10 in Finland \u2014 national section highlighted\" show=\"evbase,evfinland\" /&gt;\nHighlight: \u2501\u2501 Finnish section \u00b7 \u2501\u2501 other mapped EV10 sections.\nHaparanda/Tornio \u2013 Kemi \u2013 Kuivaniemi \u2013 Oulu \u2013 Liminka \u2013 Siikajoki \u2013 Pyh\u00e4joki \u2013 Kalajoki \u2013 Kokkola \u2013 Jakobstad \u2013 Oravais \u2013 Vaasa \u