{"id": "219363", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219363", "title": "Most", "text": "Most is a town in North Bohemia, in the Czech Republic.\nUnderstand.\nThe area around Most (the name means \"Bridge\") has been populated since the 11th century. In the 19th century, large deposits of brown coal were discovered in the area. In 1870, a train connection to this city was opened. Between 1938 and 1945, it was, as part of Sudetenland, annexed by Germany. In the 1960s, the old city was demolished due to expansion of mining, and its Gothic church was moved to a different location.\nGet in.\nBy bus.\nThere are several direct buses from Prague's Florenc to Most 08:00-21:00. The trips take from 1 hr 35 min to 2 hr 20 min and cost between 109 and 138 K\u010d for adults.\nBy train.\nMost has a direct train connection from Prague and Plze\u0148. The ride from Prague takes 2 hours. From Plze\u0148, it takes about 2 hr 40 min.\nGet around.\nThe town can be comfortably covered on foot. There are also city buses and trams, the trams also go to neighbouring town of Litv\u00ednov."}
{"id": "219382", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219382", "title": "Dufftown", "text": "Dufftown is a village in Moray in northeast Scotland, with a population in 2022 of 1500. Together with the villages of Craigellachie and Rothes to the north, it has a formidable collection of whisky distilleries, and many can be toured. \nUnderstand.\nWhisky production in Scotland is first recorded in 1494. Until the 19th century it was little better than Caledonian cough-mixture - gentlemen drank wine and brandy, and the hoi polloi got wasted on gin. Then the Napoleonic wars cut off supply from the continent, distillation of spirits became regulated by the Excise Act of 1823, and there was a crackdown on \"moonshine\". Several places claim to be the oldest licensed distillery in Scotland and all of them are correct: they were already in business when the Act came into force. The trade was boosted in 1831 by the invention of continuous distillation columns, from 1860 by blending (which creates a smoother, more palatable drink) and from 1880 by the \"phylloxera\" blight, which ravaged wine- and brandy-making. Whisky production industrialised from small stills on farms to great factories with their pagoda roofs and mash-tun aroma, supplying Britain and its thirsty colonies.\nAccidents of geography, but mostly a good supply of pure water and of malted barley, created a concentration of distilleries in several towns, and one such is Dufftown near the foot of the Spey glen. It was said to be \"founded upon seven stills\" - an allusion to ancient Rome - and nowadays a dozen are active here. Most output is blended, but the premier product is single malt. This can be sampled in \"nips\" or \"drams\" in the pub, by the bottle from the supermarket or wine-shop, on distillery tours, or through curated whisky-tastings. Pace yourself: the average output from each distillery is 5 million litres per annum, so do the maths and it's all got to be swallowed somehow. Your consumption however modest will be appreciated.\nGet in.\nBy road from Aberdeen follow A96 to Huntly then A920 west, 52 miles.\nStagecoach Bus 36 runs every two hours M-Sa from Elgin, taking an hour via Craigellachie.\nMoray Council Bus 368 runs four times M-F from Tomintoul (30 min), and two of them continue east to Keith (another 30 min).\nElgin is the nearest practical railway station, with trains between Aberdeen and Inverness every hour or two. On summer weekends you can get off at Keith and take the heritage railway to Dufftown.\nGet around.\nSeveral attractions are within walking distance of Dufftown. You need your own wheels to tour the wider area, but think about who's driving after the whisky tasting.\nAce Taxis are on +44 7383 820820.\n Isla Way is a side-trail from Dufftown to Keith. \n Dufftown FC play further down in the amateur leagues. \n Rothes GC is west side of Rothes village. Green tees 5133 yards, par 69. \nConnect.\nAs of Aug 2026 Dufftown and its approach roads have 4G from EE, Three and Vodafone, but only a poor patchy signal from O2. 5G has not yet reached the village."}
{"id": "219426", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219426", "title": "Cupar", "text": "Cupar is a historic\u00a0market town that is the largest settlement in Fife, in the north east of Scotland.\nUnderstand.\nThe historic town centre is the junction of Bonnygate and the Crossgate. This is where the town's\u00a0mercat cross is located. Its shaft is original, and is supported by a unicorn. It dates from 1683.\nIn 2022, it had a population of 8,800.\nDrink.\nPubs in Cupar are mainly just for drinking with not much food on offer."}
